1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,840
Before asking whether the patient has capacity, identify the decision:
a blood-pressure examination, an injection, a transport recommendation
or a proposed alternative plan. Consent to one does not automatically
authorize all the others.

2
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:31,152
The school Health Law module extends Week One records and
communication into difficult decisions. Its eleven sections include
the actual conversation, refusal, minors, substitutes, legal documents
and police-custody boundaries.

3
00:00:31,152 --> 00:00:45,408
Keep clinical need separate from legal authority. An intervention can
be clinically indicated while consent or another lawful basis remains
unresolved; a signed form cannot make a contraindicated intervention
appropriate.

4
00:00:45,408 --> 00:00:58,320
Assess, explain, listen and document, then revisit when circumstances
change. The desired result is a valid patient-centred decision, not
simply agreement with the clinician.

5
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:12,456
An adult may express a choice in words or writing, and relevant
conduct may communicate agreement. Explain the examination before
interpreting an extended arm as consent to a cuff; it is not consent
to an injection.

6
00:01:12,456 --> 00:01:26,424
A signature records an event but does not prove that the information
was understood or that the person was free from pressure. A verbal
decision can be valid without a routine bedside signature when the
actual rules permit it.

7
00:01:26,424 --> 00:01:41,616
An unconscious person who needs immediate care presents a different
legal problem. The school sometimes groups that under implied consent
and elsewhere calls it an emergency exception; do not treat the word
implied as a complete legal analysis.

8
00:01:41,616 --> 00:01:52,800
Use the actual lawful emergency pathway, known wishes and available
authority, with clinical support when needed. Return decision-making
to the patient when they can participate.

9
00:01:52,800 --> 00:02:04,176
Explain the condition or concern in plain language, what you propose
and the intended benefit. A drug name alone tells the patient very
little about why you want to administer it.

10
00:02:04,176 --> 00:02:18,048
Discuss material risks and reasonably available alternatives,
including no intervention when relevant. Avoid both a falsely
reassuring guarantee and an exhaustive list that buries the important
choice.

11
00:02:18,048 --> 00:02:29,640
Explain foreseeable consequences of declining with the same honesty as
benefits. Tailor the discussion to the current concern instead of
saying you could die to every person as a threat.

12
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:41,304
Invite questions and check understanding in the patient’s own words.
Correct a misunderstanding, then ask again; consent remains a
conversation and can be withdrawn.

13
00:02:41,304 --> 00:02:51,936
An original opening is: I recommend this medicine for the symptom we
discussed. I will explain how it may help, the important side effects
and the other options before you decide.

14
00:02:51,936 --> 00:03:04,368
Then supply the medication-specific information, allergy and
contraindication checks. A generic consent script cannot replace
knowing the actual medicine and the risks relevant to this person.

15
00:03:04,368 --> 00:03:16,728
Ask: What do you understand the medicine is for, and what concerns or
questions do you have? This is a check on the explanation, not an
examination requiring the patient to use medical vocabulary.

16
00:03:16,728 --> 00:03:28,800
Record the specific agreement or refusal and any limits. If the
patient later declines another dose or route, reassess that new
decision instead of relying on the earlier yes.

17
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:41,544
A person who cannot hear your explanation has not failed a capacity
test. Face the patient, check preferred communication and use
appropriate hearing, language or alternative-communication support.

18
00:03:41,544 --> 00:03:54,936
Use an appropriate interpreter where available, especially for a high-
stakes decision. A family member may help with the patient’s
agreement, but can also introduce pressure, omissions or a privacy
concern.

19
00:03:54,936 --> 00:04:08,232
Pain, fear and the setting can reduce effective participation. A
calmer explanation and symptom support may improve understanding; do
not assume that every distressed person is incapable.

20
00:04:08,232 --> 00:04:21,816
Document what support was used and what the person then demonstrated.
Aphasia, disability, limited English and an unusual communication
style are not stand-alone reasons to take control away.

21
00:04:21,816 --> 00:04:33,312
Presume adult capability unless evidence requires closer assessment.
Ask the patient to describe the concern, the offered care and what
accepting or declining might mean for them.

22
00:04:33,312 --> 00:04:48,552
The school describes understanding, retention, use or weighing and
communication. These are useful clinical prompts, but the BC adult Act
section seven is worded around understanding the information and its
application to the person.

23
00:04:48,552 --> 00:05:01,848
Capacity is a clinical judgment tied to a decision and time. The
school contrasts it with court-determined competence; do not use a
broad court label or diagnosis to skip the current clinical
assessment.

24
00:05:01,848 --> 00:05:15,672
A normal GCS, orientation answers or agreement with you is not
sufficient evidence by itself. Conversely, disagreement or an
unconventional value does not prove inability to decide.

25
00:05:15,672 --> 00:05:31,272
Hypoxia, hypoglycemia, delirium, intoxication and head injury can
impair decisions. Identify both the clinical concern and its actual
effect on understanding rather than simply assigning incapacity from a
list.

26
00:05:31,272 --> 00:05:46,464
One person with alcohol exposure may understand the proposed care;
another cannot follow or apply the information. Alcohol odour does not
answer the clinical question, and it must not obscure an injury or
metabolic emergency.

27
00:05:46,464 --> 00:06:02,424
Correct reversible threats using the appropriate authority and
pathway, then reassess. After glucose correction or naloxone response,
do not assume that one improved number or a sudden awakening
automatically restores every decision-making function.

28
00:06:02,424 --> 00:06:16,224
Risk changes the care needed in the assessment, not the patient’s
obligation to choose your preferred option. Document the specific
responses and remaining uncertainty, and obtain support for a high-
risk unresolved decision.

29
00:06:16,224 --> 00:06:30,816
The school two-stage model asks about a disturbance and then its
effect on understanding, memory, weighing and communication. It helps
prevent a reflex conclusion, but it is not the verbatim test in the BC
statute.

30
00:06:30,816 --> 00:06:43,992
Use the model to explain clinical reasoning without claiming that a
specific diagnostic label is a statutory prerequisite. The clinically
relevant question remains what the patient can demonstrate for the
proposed decision.

31
00:06:43,992 --> 00:06:56,616
Do not test with trivia unrelated to care. Knowing the date does not
prove understanding of an occult-bleeding risk, and forgetting a date
does not necessarily prove inability to choose a simple examination.

32
00:06:56,616 --> 00:07:08,088
Record the question, the supported answer and why it does or does not
demonstrate understanding. This is stronger evidence than oriented
times four, therefore competent.

33
00:07:08,088 --> 00:07:23,664
In this school module, VIRCA means voluntary, informed, relevant,
capacity and advice. Retain that exact expansion for its classroom
terminology, while recognizing that a mnemonic does not replace
legislation or policy.

34
00:07:23,664 --> 00:07:37,224
Check whether the decision is free from pressure and whether you have
explained the actual concern, benefits, material risks and
alternatives. Ask for the patient’s understanding rather than a yes to
do you understand.

35
00:07:37,224 --> 00:07:51,672
Relevant means the choice concerns the current proposed care. It does
not mean the clinician must agree that the choice is wise. Confirm the
scope: declining an injection may not mean declining examination or
transport.

36
00:07:51,672 --> 00:08:02,736
Demonstrate the capability finding and provide specific follow-up and
worsening-symptom advice. Advice must be practical for the person’s
mobility, supports and access to help.

37
00:08:02,736 --> 00:08:15,288
The module expands ACDC as autonomy, comprehension, disclosure and
capacity. It is an alternate organizing aid, not a separate legal
licence to override a refusal.

38
00:08:15,288 --> 00:08:27,168
Autonomy asks whose decision this is and whether a substitute or
another lawful authority actually applies. Do not accept a relative’s
preference simply because they speak more forcefully.

39
00:08:27,168 --> 00:08:38,976
Comprehension and disclosure form a two-way check. Poor understanding
may mean your explanation or communication support was inadequate, not
that the patient is incapable.

40
00:08:38,976 --> 00:08:51,672
Capacity records the actual evidence for the relevant choice. You do
not need to repeat multiple acronyms until the patient gives the
answer you want; repair missing information and seek support when
needed.

41
00:08:51,672 --> 00:09:02,952
The school PLANE aid asks whether an action is proportionate, legal,
accountable, necessary and ethical. Use it to challenge an
overreaching plan under pressure.

42
00:09:02,952 --> 00:09:15,840
Proportionate and necessary require the actual threat and a targeted
response. A genuine need for hemorrhage control does not automatically
justify every other examination or coercive measure.

43
00:09:15,840 --> 00:09:28,656
Legal requires an applicable authority, not merely the belief that a
good outcome is likely. Calling a clinician or police does not
retroactively create an authority that neither role possesses.

44
00:09:28,656 --> 00:09:44,712
Accountability requires the evidence, alternatives considered, urgency
and reassessment to be recorded. Ethical care preserves autonomy where
possible and minimizes intrusion; it is not a rule that beneficence
always defeats refusal.

45
00:09:44,712 --> 00:09:58,608
First clarify exactly what is being refused and why. Fear, cost
assumptions, a dependent person at home or an earlier harmful
encounter may reveal a solvable barrier without pressuring the
patient.

46
00:09:58,608 --> 00:10:10,104
Complete the permitted clinical assessment and explain significant
uncertainty. If the patient refuses part of it, document that limit
and its effect on your ability to assess risk.

47
00:10:10,104 --> 00:10:23,328
Consult where required or where you need help with a safe plan. A
suggested alternate service must actually be available and
appropriate; saying see a doctor is not the same as an accepted
referral.

48
00:10:23,328 --> 00:10:36,912
Respect a capable informed refusal while providing appropriate advice
and documenting the process. A release signature does not erase the
duty to assess, communicate and make the encounter as safe as
possible.

49
00:10:36,912 --> 00:10:53,832
The school lists high-risk complaints, impairment, pregnancy, abnormal
ECG and physiological triggers for consultation, including the
numerical examples displayed. Learn these as the assigned school list,
not as a verified complete current service list.

50
00:10:53,832 --> 00:11:08,640
Its older-age wording itself varies between over sixty-five and
seventy, so do not quietly choose one exact threshold as a universal
rule. The actual current policy must resolve a real operational
threshold.

51
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:24,264
Public A nine explicitly requires consultation before pediatric and
police-custody non-conveyance. The documentation standard points to a
longer current list whose full body is access restricted; it has not
been guessed or bypassed.

52
00:11:24,264 --> 00:11:39,864
Uncertainty, a red flag or inability to explain the actual refusal
risk is reason to seek support. Consultation supports a safer plan; it
does not manufacture consent or independently authorize forced
transport.

53
00:11:39,864 --> 00:11:56,928
Urgent treatment without expressed consent must have the applicable
lawful basis. The adult Act describes an emergency exception with
conditions, including urgency, incapability, unavailable authorized
representation and a second opinion where practicable.

54
00:11:56,928 --> 00:12:13,512
Do not simply claim that every EMA personally has every power in that
Act. Its provider definition and regulation specify professional
classes; R four itself warns that paramedics cannot independently
appoint a temporary substitute under that scheme.

55
00:12:13,512 --> 00:12:28,800
Use the actual emergency-care law, professional authority and service
pathway with appropriate clinical support. A known applicable refusal
or directive matters; a needed intervention is not a blanket
permission for unrelated care.

56
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:42,432
Document why immediate action was required, the capability finding,
available information and the authority relied on. Reassess promptly
and obtain a valid patient or authorized decision when it becomes
possible.

57
00:12:42,432 --> 00:12:56,904
BC adulthood begins at nineteen, but the Infants Act does not set a
universal minimum consent birthday. Its test addresses understanding
of the proposed care and the provider’s best-interests conclusion
after reasonable efforts.

58
00:12:56,904 --> 00:13:14,328
Explain nature, consequences and reasonably foreseeable benefits and
risks, then establish what this child or adolescent understands.
Living with parents does not by itself disqualify a young person, and
independent living does not by itself prove understanding.

59
00:13:14,328 --> 00:13:28,152
If statutory consent criteria are met, a second parental consent is
not automatically required for that care. Verify the role of any
guardian and respect appropriate privacy while offering useful
support.

60
00:13:28,152 --> 00:13:44,928
Do not stretch a consent provision into an absolute answer to every
life-threatening refusal conflict. High-risk disagreements and
pediatric non-conveyance need the appropriate clinical and legal
pathway; the public A nine consultation requirement still applies.

61
00:13:44,928 --> 00:13:55,416
Do not move to a substitute just because a family member is present or
the conversation is difficult. Make reasonable efforts to support the
patient’s own decision first.

62
00:13:55,416 --> 00:14:08,640
A court-appointed committee and a representative under an agreement
have specific authority, not unlimited control. Verify the actual
document, person, scope and relevance to the proposed care.

63
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:24,792
The school lists the temporary-substitute hierarchy, but identifying a
possible relative is not the same as making a lawful appointment.
Public A nine and R four differ in wording; use eligible clinical
support rather than independently appointing someone.

64
00:14:24,792 --> 00:14:37,416
The substitute should represent the patient’s applicable known wishes
and, where required, best interests. A financially authorized power of
attorney is not automatically a health-care representative.

65
00:14:37,416 --> 00:14:50,448
The school sometimes groups these documents as advance directives.
Retain the clinical topic but not that legal equivalence: a medical
order and a legally compliant adult instruction are different
instruments.

66
00:14:50,448 --> 00:15:04,056
Check identity, the relevant care, validity and applicability. A
current capable decision remains important, and a No CPR order does
not prohibit every symptom-relief or supportive intervention.

67
00:15:04,056 --> 00:15:17,880
Do not use an absolute rule that an advance directive always wins over
every representation agreement. The adult Act has specific interaction
provisions; conflicting or uncertain documents require appropriate
direction.

68
00:15:17,880 --> 00:15:33,096
The current Emergency Health Services Act places the advance-directive
restriction at section eleven point one; the older R four page cites
eleven. Preserve the meaning and correct source locator instead of
teaching obsolete numbering as current.

69
00:15:33,096 --> 00:15:47,640
A psychiatric history must not overshadow a medical cause of agitation
or confusion. Assess airway, breathing, circulation, glucose, injury,
exposures and other indicated findings.

70
00:15:47,640 --> 00:16:04,848
Neither a mental-health diagnosis nor refusal alone establishes
incapability. Conversely, a physically uninjured person may still have
an urgent mental-health need; do not create a false rule that only
visible physical injury can justify urgent care.

71
00:16:04,848 --> 00:16:19,368
Identify the actual consent or statutory transport pathway. De-
escalation, voluntary participation, scene safety and timely
appropriate help remain important whether or not an apprehension power
applies.

72
00:16:19,368 --> 00:16:33,312
The school’s implied-consent warning is a useful boundary against
overreach, but its physical-versus-psychiatric shorthand is not the
whole law. Do not infer a coercive power from a clinical label or an
uncomfortable risk.

73
00:16:33,312 --> 00:16:45,360
Section twenty-eight gives a police officer or constable a defined
apprehension role when the statutory criteria are met. Paramedics
cannot simply announce that they have enacted that section.

74
00:16:45,360 --> 00:17:00,696
A qualifying section twenty-two certificate has a separate statutory
effect for apprehension and transportation. Current first-certificate
wording includes a physician or nurse practitioner; the school
physician-only shorthand is incomplete.

75
00:17:00,696 --> 00:17:15,312
Verify the actual valid instrument and care pathway rather than a
casual assertion that a form exists. A diagnosis, an unsigned draft or
an employer preference is not equivalent to the statutory conditions.

76
00:17:15,312 --> 00:17:31,248
Clarify roles and safe transport with appropriate clinical and police
support. This lesson does not teach restraint techniques or confer a
new power; document who exercised which actual authority and what
clinical care was accepted or declined.

77
00:17:31,248 --> 00:17:42,264
A patient in police custody remains a patient. Custody alone does not
authorize an IV, drug or examination against a capable valid refusal.

78
00:17:42,264 --> 00:17:56,832
Transport and treatment can have different lawful bases, but do not
say that any police or organizational request automatically removes
all transport rights. Verify the actual authority and who is
exercising it.

79
00:17:56,832 --> 00:18:11,592
Maintain independent clinical priorities and communicate any
restriction that interferes with examination, breathing or monitoring.
Escalate a safety conflict rather than accepting a nonclinical
instruction that harms care.

80
00:18:11,592 --> 00:18:26,184
Document the patient’s actual choice, clinical findings, custody
status, responsible officers and any separate transport authority.
Current A nine requires consultation before non-conveyance in this
population.

81
00:18:26,184 --> 00:18:38,232
Clinical information remains private in a custody encounter. A uniform
or an ongoing investigation is not, by itself, an unlimited
entitlement to the whole patient record.

82
00:18:38,232 --> 00:18:51,288
Identify the request and applicable authority, consent or specific
exception. Urgent safety information may need appropriate sharing, but
do not label every investigative interest an emergency.

83
00:18:51,288 --> 00:19:04,224
Share only the appropriate necessary information and obtain privacy or
clinical support where uncertainty permits. Do not use a personal
phone or an unapproved messaging channel to send patient details.

84
00:19:04,224 --> 00:19:15,504
Record what was shared, with whom and why. The record should
distinguish patient consent from another lawful disclosure basis
rather than treating police presence as consent.

85
00:19:15,504 --> 00:19:28,104
A useful record tells the reviewer what the patient understood and how
you know. Quote a short relevant response or summarize its substance
accurately instead of only writing has capacity.

86
00:19:28,104 --> 00:19:45,672
The current refusal documentation standard requests history,
examination and two complete observation sets, with explanations when
requirements cannot be met. A patient may decline assessment; that
limit must be documented, not filled with invented normals.

87
00:19:45,672 --> 00:20:02,112
Record the exact scope of refusal, information and alternatives
discussed, consultation and specific safety advice. If the patient or
witness refuses to sign, document that; lack of a signature does not
itself prove incapability.

88
00:20:02,112 --> 00:20:14,136
A consultation record includes the clinician, time, material findings,
advice and what was actually done. Document continuing or restored
patient participation when the condition changes.

89
00:20:14,136 --> 00:20:27,792
An adult becomes awake after ventilation and indicated naloxone, then
asks you to leave. Do not jump from awakening to a global capacity
conclusion or from possible opioid use to automatic incapability.

90
00:20:27,792 --> 00:20:43,872
Reassess sustained breathing, circulation, glucose and other indicated
concerns. Explain the risk of recurrence and why continued observation
or transport is recommended, using the actual case rather than a
generic threat.

91
00:20:43,872 --> 00:20:56,688
Ask the patient to explain that concern and how it applies to them.
Address communication barriers and explore the reason for refusal;
involve appropriate support under the current high-risk pathway.

92
00:20:56,688 --> 00:21:09,048
Document the evidence and the actual decision. An initial medicine
response neither creates permission to force every later treatment nor
makes a high-risk departure automatically safe.

93
00:21:09,048 --> 00:21:22,224
A fifteen-year-old living with parents explains the proposed
examination and its likely benefit and risk. The living arrangement
alone does not answer whether the Infants Act consent criteria are
met.

94
00:21:22,224 --> 00:21:34,056
Explain the specific care, confirm actual understanding and make the
required best-interests assessment. Do not substitute the school
example’s independence traits for the statute’s criteria.

95
00:21:34,056 --> 00:21:48,312
Appropriate parental involvement can support care, but is not
automatically a second veto when valid minor consent applies. Verify
privacy and guardianship issues rather than promising absolute
confidentiality.

96
00:21:48,312 --> 00:22:01,008
If a high-risk refusal or disagreement emerges, obtain the appropriate
clinical and legal support. The course does not resolve every
pediatric dispute by one age threshold or one mnemonic.

97
00:22:01,008 --> 00:22:16,416
An adult in custody demonstrates understanding of an IV proposal and
declines it. Police state that transport is required under a
particular custody authority. Assess and verify these as two separate
questions.

98
00:22:16,416 --> 00:22:29,712
An authorized transport does not automatically authorize the refused
IV. Explain the clinical need, alternatives and consequences without
asking police to replace a capable healthcare decision.

99
00:22:29,712 --> 00:22:43,224
Clarify the actual transport authority, responsible officers,
destination and monitoring access. If roles or safety conflict, seek
appropriate support and document the unresolved concern.

100
00:22:43,224 --> 00:22:55,200
Use a factual record of what was declined, the demonstrated
understanding, what authority governed movement and what care
occurred. Do not write a custody label as a substitute for that
evidence.

101
00:22:55,200 --> 00:23:09,816
An incapable adult has a MOST and a representation agreement with
apparently conflicting instructions. A relative asks you to follow
their preference. First identify the instruments and authority, not
who speaks loudest.

102
00:23:09,816 --> 00:23:21,840
Check that the documents belong to the patient and apply to the
current decision. A medical order, a representation agreement and an
advance directive have different roles and interaction rules.

103
00:23:21,840 --> 00:23:35,568
Seek appropriate clinical direction rather than inventing a universal
hierarchy or independently appointing a relative. Do not withhold all
comfort or necessary supportive care solely because the discussion is
complex.

104
00:23:35,568 --> 00:23:48,816
Record the information available, the advice, the applicable
instructions and the reason for actions. This demonstrates accountable
decision-making under uncertainty without falsely resolving the legal
dispute.

105
00:23:48,816 --> 00:24:00,456
In a station, name the particular decision and proposed care, identify
who can decide, show what understanding was demonstrated and state the
legal or consent basis for your action.

106
00:24:00,456 --> 00:24:14,232
Then explain the care plan, safety advice and reassessment. A high-
quality response integrates clinical risk with autonomy rather than
choosing a slogan such as always transport or always respect any
refusal.

107
00:24:14,232 --> 00:24:26,760
Use the school glossary and frameworks for the assigned terminology,
with the source qualifications in the paired reading. Recognition of a
mnemonic is not evidence that the underlying decision was valid.

108
00:24:26,760 --> 00:24:41,088
The later medication-administration session uses the same process
before a route or dose is given. Consent, clinical indication, dose
accuracy and professional authorization are separate checks that all
need attention.
